35:## Decision
43:## Merged evidence, in stage order
47:| T1, IRWB-020-B | A native AST evaluator and FP comparators feed a seeded meaning gate against origin. Inline counts uses, substitutes affine indices and deletes the producer. Split builds block/tail reductions. The graph exposes function and tensor entities, plus named reads, and leaves editing unsupported. `src/tensor/contract.ts:24`, `src/tensor/contract.ts:100`, `src/tensor/contract.ts:121`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:16`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:53`, `src/tensor/ir.ts:102`, `src/tensor/ir.ts:140`. | The evaluator, arithmetic, and affine substitution remain language semantics. Reuse use-def analysis, guarded single-use inlining, scoped matching, and a native edit receipt. |
56:## Assessment by layer
64:| **Dataflow** | Keep the forward/backward worklist, meet/join interface, boundary facts, and declared read-set transfer for CFG problems. Typed liveness, reaching definitions and range propagation are concrete users. `src/framework/dataflow/solver.ts:20`, `src/framework/dataflow/analysis.ts:120`, `src/framework/dataflow/analysis.ts:160`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:296`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:352`, `src/typed/analyses.ts:486`. | In the merged Tix path, the adapter emits tensor and read edges, and optimization and scheduling consume native ASTs. I found no use of the dataflow solver or directed rewrite runner there. `src/tensor/ir.ts:102`, `src/tensor/passes.ts:82`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:142`, `src/tensor/index.ts:32`. A CFG is a useful solver input. It is a poor requirement for affine dependence or a changing set of abstract specialization states. Add reusable graph analyses, sparse/worklist services where a second client exists, and a sibling dependence provider. Tix’s tensor use counting and dependence logic presently live in AST traversal and fusion. `src/tensor/passes.ts:23`, `src/tensor/fusion.ts:69`. |
